      "aLevel": {"status":"verified","accepted":"yes","detail":"The school transcript should include results of external examinations such as A Levels that are required for university entrance in the home country; MIT asks for predicted grades if available. A Levels scores are self-reported in the application’s Test scores section (official scores are not required at application).","sources":[{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/international/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — International applicants"},{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/tests-scores/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Tests & scores"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":["Your high school transcript should include three years of grades and the results of any external examinations such as the International Baccalaureate, A Levels, French Baccalaureate, or CBSE, which are required for entrance to universities in your home country. We understand that many grades are not available until after decisions are made and ask that predicted grades be sent if available.","Students should self-report scores for standardized exams they have taken or plan to take, such as Advanced Placement, International Baccalaureate, A-level, Baccalauréat, etc., on the Test scores section of the application. We do not require applicants to officially send scores as part of their application, and instead have a verification process upon enrollment."],"note":"MIT does not convert grades to the American system and states no A-Level grade or IB points requirement. Only admitted students are required to send final grades."},
      "ib": {"status":"verified","accepted":"yes","detail":"The school transcript should include results of external examinations such as the International Baccalaureate that are required for university entrance in the home country; MIT asks for predicted grades if available. the International Baccalaureate scores are self-reported in the application’s Test scores section (official scores are not required at application).","sources":[{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/international/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — International applicants"},{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/tests-scores/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Tests & scores"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":["Your high school transcript should include three years of grades and the results of any external examinations such as the International Baccalaureate, A Levels, French Baccalaureate, or CBSE, which are required for entrance to universities in your home country. We understand that many grades are not available until after decisions are made and ask that predicted grades be sent if available.","Students should self-report scores for standardized exams they have taken or plan to take, such as Advanced Placement, International Baccalaureate, A-level, Baccalauréat, etc., on the Test scores section of the application. We do not require applicants to officially send scores as part of their application, and instead have a verification process upon enrollment."],"note":"MIT does not convert grades to the American system and states no A-Level grade or IB points requirement. Only admitted students are required to send final grades."},
      "otherQualifications": {"status":"verified","items":["Advanced Placement and other exam scores (e.g. IB, A-level, Baccalauréat): self-reported in the Test scores section of the application","Official score reports are not required at application; scores are verified upon enrollment"],"sources":[{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/tests-scores/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Tests & scores"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":"Students should self-report scores for standardized exams they have taken or plan to take, such as Advanced Placement, International Baccalaureate, A-level, Baccalauréat, etc., on the Test scores section of the application. We do not require applicants to officially send scores as part of their application, and instead have a verification process upon enrollment."},
      "otherRequirements": {"status":"verified","items":["Interview: offered by an MIT Educational Counselor when one is available; if none is available it is waived without disadvantage","Creative portfolios (research, music & theater arts, visual art & architecture, maker): optional","Two teacher letters of recommendation required (one math/science and one humanities/social science/language recommended)"],"sources":[{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/interview/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Interview"},{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/portfolios-additional-material/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Creative portfolios"},{"url":"https://mitadmissions.org/apply/firstyear/deadlines-requirements/","label":"MIT: First-year applicants — Deadlines & requirements"}],"lastVerified":"2026-10-19","sourceSnippet":["After you submit your application, you may be contacted by an Educational Counselor (EC) if there’s one available. … If we are unable to offer you an interview, it will be waived, and your application will not be adversely affected.","Researchers, performing artists, visual artists, and makers may submit optional portfolios for review by MIT staff or faculty through SlideRoom.","Letters of recommendation: We require two letters of recommendation from teachers, which you can request through the application. We recommend one letter from a math or science teacher, and one from a humanities, social science, or language teacher."]}
